The '''1999–2000 season''' was the 106th season of competitive [[1999–2000 in Scottish football|football]] by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]. Celtic competed in the [[1999–2000 Scottish Premier League|Scottish Premier League]], [[1999–2000 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]], [[1999–2000 Scottish League Cup|Scottish League Cup]] and the [[1999–2000 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup]].

==Summary==
The season saw Celtic finish second in the league, 21 points behind the winners, [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://scottishfootballarchive.co.uk/league-tables/1999-00-premier-league |title=1999-00 Premier League |publisher=Scottish Football Archive |access-date=17 November 2011 }}{{dead link|date=June 2017 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> They won the League Cup beating [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] in the final,<ref>{{cite web | url=http://scottishfootballarchive.co.uk/fixtures/1999-00-league-cup | title=1999-00 League Cup | publisher=Scottish Football Archive | access-date=17 November 2011 | archive-url=https://archive.today/20130421051952/http://scottishfootballarchive.co.uk/fixtures/1999-00-league-cup | archive-date=21 April 2013 | url-status=dead}}</ref> and reached the second round of the UEFA Cup, losing to [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/history/clubs/50050--celtic/ | title=1999/00 | publisher=Uefa Cuo | access-date=17 November 2011}}</ref> They were knocked out of the Scottish Cup in the third round by [[Inverness Caledonian Thistle F.C.|Inverness Caledonian Thistle]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.londonhearts.com/scores/sc/1999-00.html | title=Scottish Cup Results for 1999-00 | publisher=London Hearts | access-date=17 November 2011}}</ref>

